  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry today with increasing humidity. \nLow risk for early morning showers across the Islands.\n\nA weak high pressure over western CT has brought mostly clear \nskies and calm winds to southern New England, leading to a \nlittle bit of river valley fog and dropping temperatures to \naround 60 across the usual radiator sites. A weak low pressure \nsystem has spun up over our southern waters tonight, leading to \nsome scattered showers forming over the far southeastern waters,\nand there remains a low chance for those showers to push inland\ntowards Nantucket through the early morning hours.\n\nThe high pressure should linger near NYC today, leading to \ngenerally dry conditions for most of southern New England. The \nlow will slowly meander further east and offshore today as well.\nOnce the low moves out to sea, winds will become predominately \nsoutherly, pushing warm, moist air onshore and dewpoints will \nbegin to slowly tick up throughout today... likely approaching \n70 by this evening. Otherwise, temperatures today remain in the \nmid to upper 80s with plenty of sunshine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Summer heat and humidity returns Thursday \nthrough the weekend with chances for diurnal scattered \nthunderstorms possible each day.\n\nA Bermuda high will begin to build in across the western \nAtlantic Ocean and as southerly flow continues through the \nnight, dew points begin to surge into the mid to upper 70s by \nThursday. 925 mb temps surge to near +22C each afternoon this \nweek, leading to high temps near 90 Thursday through Saturday. \nCombined with near oppressive dew points of 72-76, \"feels like\" \ntemperatures will climb to the upper 90s, possibly near 100 at \ntimes. Heat headlines are being considered through the second \nhalf of the week and possibly into the weekend; however, some \nuncertainty remains in exactly how hot southern New England \ncould get with daily threats for diurnal thunderstorms.\n\nRegarding the thunderstorm potential later in the week... a few\nweak, upper level disturbances passing through New England \ncombining with the high heat/humidity and just enough \ninstability will likely be enough to pop a few diurnal \nshowers/thunderstorms each day Thursday through the weekend, \nwith greatest chances across the interior on Thursday. Any \nthunderstorms that do pop have the potential to produce frequent\nlightning, gusty winds, and torrential downpours as PWATs will \nlikely be around 2.0\". Given the amount of rain southern New \nEngland has seen over the last week or so, any heavy downpours \ncould lead to localized flooding.",
